From: Any OS
Subject: [PATCH] airconv: mesh workload groups for tessellation; primitives from every mesh thread

Problem: the tessellation emulation ran one 32-thread mesh threadgroup per
"workload" (one ring edge pair of a patch, ~20 vertices at the usual
factors), with thread 0 walking the primitives alone.  Measured on Unigine
Heaven 4.0 (DX11, High, tessellation Normal, 1920x1080, M1 Max): ~14 ms of
the 23 ms GPU frame was tessellation and the cost grew linearly with the
tessellation factor, i.e. with the number of workloads, not with the number
of triangles -- the per-threadgroup cost dominated (launch, mesh output
allocation, 1-2 vertex rounds with most of the 32 threads idle).

Fix:
1. Mesh workload groups.  After every patch of the object threadgroup has
   added its workloads (barrier now also fences object_data: the packer reads
   what other threads wrote), one thread packs consecutive workloads into
   groups that fit the mesh output (dxmt.pack_workload_groups writes a
   group_start table into a new payload field) and dispatches one mesh
   threadgroup per group.  The mesh function reads its [first, last) range,
   maps the concatenated vertex index to (workload, local vertex)
   (dxmt.locate_group_vertex; patch constants / control points are set up
   per vertex because a group may span patches) and emits every workload's
   primitives at running vertex / primitive bases from every thread
   (dxmt.generate_group_primitives.*).  Both stages derive the same plan
   from the same inputs (get_mesh_group_plan): rasterized triangle output of
   the tri and quad domains only; isolines, point / line output, streamed
   pipelines (geometry shader behind tessellation, stream output -- the hull
   variant now receives SM50_SHADER_TESSELLATION_STREAM too) and pass-through
   geometry shaders with per-primitive render-target / viewport array index
   keep one workload per threadgroup; grids above 1023 workloads as well
   (group bounds travel in 10-bit fields).
2. Capacity.  A single workload pair can hold 2 * factor + 10 vertices and
   2 * factor + 6 primitives (an inner edge of 3 with two outer edges of
   `factor`, gen_workload_triangle_impl's r + 1 == u case: 138 / 134 at 64),
   more than the edge-point formula the mesh declared (133 at 64); the group
   capacity, the grouped mesh declaration and estimate_mesh_size (which
   picks the pipeline's potential factor) use the larger count.  The quad
   workload bound gains the centre workload (65 at factor 64, not 64).
3. Single-workload path (streams, points, lines): the primitives are emitted
   from every mesh thread as well (slot tid, tid + 32, ...: the same slots,
   vertex order and provoking vertex as the serial walk, half-precision split
   points unchanged).  The thread index travels in bits 16.. of
   workload_index: a separately linked helper that took it as an argument
   crashed the AGX backend compiler (SIGSEGV in FPPassManager, every
   tessellation PSO failing), the same-translation-unit always_inline helpers
   do not.
4. air_builder MemFlags::ObjectData was 4 (= Texture); mem_object_data is 16.

Verification (2026-10-03, guest Windows 11 ARM64, host M1 Max):
- Unigine Heaven windowed 1920x1080 DX11 High tess Normal: 38.5 / 37.7 fps
  before -> 48.1 / 47.5 fps (48.2 / 49.1 one build earlier); fullscreen 47.0-48.4 -> 62.0 fps.
  Transient detector (40 s, t>20): 0.  Parallels 20 on the same host and
  settings: 46.1 fps windowed.
- d3d11-tess-probe hw vs WARP: 17/19 before and after (the two isolines
  cases fail against the WARP reference before as well); quad-level-64
  byte-identical to the reference.
- Shader cache: both digests bumped (0x4f720030 hull, 0x4f720031 domain) --
  cached object / mesh functions from either side of this change must not
  be paired.

